!deck: Math::Multivariable-Calculus
!type: Basic Math
!tags: Math Calculus Multivariable

!id: rFVZZKicBE
!front: State the vector equation of a line.
!back:
\[ \mathbf{r} = \mathbf{p} + t\mathbf{d} \]

where:

- \( \mathbf{r} \) is the position vector of any point on the line,
- \( \mathbf{p} \) is the position vector of a specific point on the line,
- \( \mathbf{d} \) is a nonzero direction vector of the line, and
- \( t \) is a scalar parameter.
~~~

!id: VPzGd3VPHw
!front: Why must the direction vector \( \mathbf{d} \) in a line's vector equation \( \mathbf{r} = \mathbf{p} + t\mathbf{d} \) be nonzero?
!back: If \( \mathbf{d} = \mathbf{0} \), then \( \mathbf{r} = \mathbf{p} \) for every \( t \), so the equation degenerates to the single point \( \mathbf{p} \) rather than tracing out a line. A nonzero \( \mathbf{d} \) is what gives the line an actual direction to travel along as \( t \) varies.
~~~

!id: STqbUQSLfv
!front: State the parametric equation of a line.
!back:
\[
\begin{align*}
  x &= p_x + td_x \\
  y &= p_y + td_y \\
  z &= p_z + td_z
\end{align*}
\]

where:

- \( (p_x, p_y, p_z) \) are the coordinates of the specific point on the line,
- \( (d_x, d_y, d_z) \) are the components of the nonzero direction vector, and
- \( t \) is a scalar parameter.
~~~

!id: bgiWL6WRm9
!front: Compare the vector, parametric, and symmetric equations of a line in \( \mathbb{R}^3 \).
!back:
- The vector equation \( \mathbf{r}=\mathbf{p}+t\mathbf{d} \) is coordinate-free: it says every point on the line is reached by starting at \( \mathbf{p} \) and moving along the nonzero direction vector \( \mathbf{d} \).
- The parametric equations \( x=p_x+td_x \), \( y=p_y+td_y \), \( z=p_z+td_z \) are the coordinate components of the same vector equation.
- The symmetric equation eliminates \( t \), writing the same parameter value as coordinate ratios; it requires the displayed direction components in the denominators to be nonzero.
~~~

!id: wdeUegOXeA
!front: State the symmetric equation of a line.
!back:
\[ \frac{x - p_x}{d_x} = \frac{y - p_y}{d_y} = \frac{z - p_z}{d_z} \]

where:

- \( (p_x, p_y, p_z) \) are the coordinates of the specific point on the line,
- \( (d_x, d_y, d_z) \) are the components of the direction vector, with \( d_x, d_y, d_z \neq 0 \).
~~~

!id: 4cC8PoO08c
!front: Derive the symmetric equation of a line from its parametric equations.
!back:
Let:

- \( x=p_x+td_x \), \( y=p_y+td_y \), and \( z=p_z+td_z \)
- \( d_x,d_y,d_z \ne 0 \)

To prove: \( \dfrac{x-p_x}{d_x}=\dfrac{y-p_y}{d_y}=\dfrac{z-p_z}{d_z} \).

- Solve each parametric equation for \( t \):

\[
\begin{align*}
  t &= \frac{x-p_x}{d_x} \\
  t &= \frac{y-p_y}{d_y} \\
  t &= \frac{z-p_z}{d_z}.
\end{align*}
\]

- Since all three ratios equal the same parameter \( t \), they are equal to each other.
~~~

!id: WbdrFVXeYZ
!front: Is the symmetric equation of a line defined when a component of the direction vector \( \mathbf{d} \) is zero, e.g. \( d_z = 0 \)? If not, what replaces it?
!back:
No — the ratio \( \frac{z - p_z}{d_z} \) is undefined, since it divides by zero. Instead, state
\[ z = p_z \]
separately, and keep the symmetric relation only for the remaining two coordinates:
\[ \frac{x - p_x}{d_x} = \frac{y - p_y}{d_y}. \]
~~~

!id: vGe9afFUub
!front: Under what conditions do two vector equations \( \mathbf{r}=\mathbf{p}_1+t\mathbf{d}_1 \) and \( \mathbf{r}=\mathbf{p}_2+s\mathbf{d}_2 \) describe the same line?
!back:
Assume \( \mathbf{d}_1,\mathbf{d}_2 \ne \mathbf{0} \). The two equations describe the same line exactly when:

- \( \mathbf{d}_1 \) and \( \mathbf{d}_2 \) are parallel, so they determine the same direction, and
- \( \mathbf{p}_2-\mathbf{p}_1 \) is parallel to \( \mathbf{d}_1 \), so one listed point lies on the other listed line.

Equivalently, the lines have parallel direction vectors and share at least one point.
~~~

!id: jwh7DVvV1C
!front: Classify the possible relationships between two lines in \( \mathbb{R}^3 \) from vector equations \( \mathbf{r}=\mathbf{p}_1+t\mathbf{d}_1 \) and \( \mathbf{r}=\mathbf{p}_2+s\mathbf{d}_2 \).
!back:
Assume \( \mathbf{d}_1,\mathbf{d}_2 \ne \mathbf{0} \).

- If \( \mathbf{d}_1\times\mathbf{d}_2=\mathbf{0} \), the lines are parallel; they are the same line exactly when \( \mathbf{p}_2-\mathbf{p}_1 \) is parallel to \( \mathbf{d}_1 \).
- If \( \mathbf{d}_1\times\mathbf{d}_2\ne\mathbf{0} \) and \( (\mathbf{p}_2-\mathbf{p}_1)\cdot(\mathbf{d}_1\times\mathbf{d}_2)=0 \), the lines are nonparallel and coplanar, so they intersect in exactly one point.
- If \( \mathbf{d}_1\times\mathbf{d}_2\ne\mathbf{0} \) and \( (\mathbf{p}_2-\mathbf{p}_1)\cdot(\mathbf{d}_1\times\mathbf{d}_2)\ne 0 \), the lines are skew: nonparallel, nonintersecting, and not coplanar.

![Two skew lines: the direction vectors d1, d2 are nonparallel, and the connecting vector p2 - p1 is not coplanar with them](img/line_line_classification.svg)
~~~

!id: kfdQHKQK3g
!front: State the vector equation of a plane.
!back:
\[ (\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{p}) \cdot \mathbf{n} = 0 \]

where:

- \( \mathbf{r} \) is the position vector of any point on the plane,
- \( \mathbf{p} \) is the position vector of a specific point on the plane, and
- \( \mathbf{n} \) is a nonzero normal vector to the plane.

![The plane through p with normal n: r is any other point on the plane, so r - p lies in the plane and is orthogonal to n](img/vector_equation_plane.svg)
~~~

!id: AC0XzJPVzI
!front: Why is \( \mathbf{r}\cdot\mathbf{n} \) constant on the plane \( (\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{p})\cdot\mathbf{n}=0 \)?
!back:
Expanding the equation gives

\[
\begin{align*}
  (\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{p}) \cdot \mathbf{n} &= 0 \\
  \mathbf{r} \cdot \mathbf{n} - \mathbf{p} \cdot \mathbf{n} &= 0 \\
  \mathbf{r} \cdot \mathbf{n} &= \mathbf{p} \cdot \mathbf{n} \\
  \mathbf{r} \cdot \mathbf{n} &= d
\end{align*}
\]

where \( d=\mathbf{p}\cdot\mathbf{n} \). Thus every point \( \mathbf{r} \) on the plane has the same dot product with the normal vector \( \mathbf{n} \), even though \( \mathbf{r} \) may move freely in directions perpendicular to \( \mathbf{n} \).
~~~

!id: gnXL7xLK0n
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of the normal vector \( \mathbf{n} \) in the plane equation \( (\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{p}) \cdot \mathbf{n} = 0 \).
!back: \( \mathbf{n} \) is perpendicular to the plane, meaning it is orthogonal to every vector lying within the plane. Since \( \mathbf{r} - \mathbf{p} \) is a vector connecting the known point \( \mathbf{p} \) to any other point \( \mathbf{r} \) on the plane, it necessarily lies in the plane — so the equation states exactly that this vector must be orthogonal to \( \mathbf{n} \).
~~~

!id: u0gQ9pUiNB
!front: State the scalar equation of a plane.
!back:
\[ ax + by + cz = d \]

where:

- \( (a, b, c) \) are the components of the nonzero normal vector \( \mathbf{n} \),
- \( (x, y, z) \) are the coordinates of any point on the plane, and
- \( d \) is a scalar constant.
~~~

!id: URxmzbuMG0
!front: State the parametric equation of a plane.
!back:
\[ \mathbf{r} = \mathbf{p} + s \mathbf{b} + t \mathbf{c} \]

where:

- \( \mathbf{r} \) is the position vector of any point on the plane,
- \( \mathbf{p} \) is the position vector of a specific point on the plane,
- \( \mathbf{b} \) and \( \mathbf{c} \) are two non-parallel (linearly independent) vectors parallel to the plane, and
- \( s \) and \( t \) are scalar parameters.

![Starting from p, moving s steps along b and t steps along c reaches r: the parallelogram sum r = p + sb + tc](img/parametric_equation_plane.svg)
~~~

!id: VFmho3Fg3u
!front: Why does the parametric equation of a plane \( \mathbf{r} = \mathbf{p} + s\mathbf{b} + t\mathbf{c} \) require \( \mathbf{b} \) and \( \mathbf{c} \) to be non-parallel?
!back: If \( \mathbf{b} \) and \( \mathbf{c} \) were parallel, they would be linearly dependent, so \( s\mathbf{b} + t\mathbf{c} \) would only ever span a one-dimensional set of displacements from \( \mathbf{p} \) — i.e. a line, not a plane. Two non-parallel (linearly independent) vectors are needed to span the two degrees of freedom of a plane.
~~~

!id: WRyJg69XNd
!front: Convert the parametric plane \( \mathbf{r}=\mathbf{p}+s\mathbf{b}+t\mathbf{c} \), with \( \mathbf{b} \) and \( \mathbf{c} \) nonparallel, to a scalar plane equation.
!back:
Let:

- \( \mathbf{r}=\mathbf{p}+s\mathbf{b}+t\mathbf{c} \)
- \( \mathbf{b},\mathbf{c}\in\mathbb{R}^3 \) nonparallel

To find: a scalar equation for the same plane.

- Since \( \mathbf{b} \) and \( \mathbf{c} \) are nonparallel, \( \mathbf{n}=\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}\ne\mathbf{0} \).
- This \( \mathbf{n} \) is perpendicular to both spanning directions, so it is normal to the plane.
- Therefore the plane is

\[ (\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{p})\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})=0. \]

In coordinates, if \( \mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}=\langle a,b,c\rangle \), this becomes \( ax+by+cz=d \), where \( d=\mathbf{p}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}) \).

![The spanning vectors b and c give the plane's normal n = b x c](img/plane_normal_from_span.svg)
~~~

!id: 6FBDvy0Ops
!front: How do you find the plane through three points \( \mathbf{p},\mathbf{q},\mathbf{s}\in\mathbb{R}^3 \)?
!back:
If the three points are noncollinear, then

\[
\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{p}
\]

are nonparallel direction vectors in the plane. A normal vector is

\[ \mathbf{n}=(\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p})\times(\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{p}), \]

so the plane equation is

\[ (\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{p})\cdot\bigl((\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p})\times(\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{p})\bigr)=0. \]

![The plane through p, q, s: the edge vectors q - p and s - p give the normal n = (q-p) x (s-p)](img/plane_through_three_points.svg)
~~~

!id: hAuMHiIesC
!front: What breaks when trying to determine a plane from three collinear points in \( \mathbb{R}^3 \)?
!back:
If \( \mathbf{p},\mathbf{q},\mathbf{s} \) are collinear, then \( \mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p} \) and \( \mathbf{s}-\mathbf{p} \) are parallel, so

\[ (\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p})\times(\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{p})=\mathbf{0}. \]

That gives no nonzero normal vector. Geometrically, three collinear points determine only a line, and infinitely many planes contain that line.
~~~

!id: qgdNbhQIkn
!front: Classify the intersection of the line \( \mathbf{r}=\mathbf{p}+t\mathbf{d} \) with the plane \( (\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{q})\cdot\mathbf{n}=0 \).
!back:
Assume \( \mathbf{d}\ne\mathbf{0} \) and \( \mathbf{n}\ne\mathbf{0} \).

Substitute the line into the plane:

\[
(\mathbf{p}+t\mathbf{d}-\mathbf{q})\cdot\mathbf{n}=0,
\qquad
t(\mathbf{d}\cdot\mathbf{n})=(\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p})\cdot\mathbf{n}.
\]

Then:

- if \( \mathbf{d}\cdot\mathbf{n}\ne 0 \), the line intersects the plane in one point, at \( t=\dfrac{(\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p})\cdot\mathbf{n}}{\mathbf{d}\cdot\mathbf{n}} \);
- if \( \mathbf{d}\cdot\mathbf{n}=0 \) and \( (\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{q})\cdot\mathbf{n}=0 \), the whole line lies in the plane;
- if \( \mathbf{d}\cdot\mathbf{n}=0 \) and \( (\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{q})\cdot\mathbf{n}\ne 0 \), the line is parallel to the plane and does not intersect it.

![A line transversal to a plane: since d is not orthogonal to n, the line pierces the plane in exactly one point](img/line_plane_intersection.svg)
~~~

!id: pkzvs2hvju
!front: Derive the distance from a point \( \mathbf{x}_0 \) to the plane \( (\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{p})\cdot\mathbf{n}=0 \).
!back:
Let:

- \( \mathbf{x}_0\in\mathbb{R}^3 \) be the point
- \( \mathbf{p} \) be a point on the plane
- \( \mathbf{n}\ne\mathbf{0} \) be a normal vector to the plane

To prove: \( \operatorname{dist}(\mathbf{x}_0,\Pi)=\dfrac{|(\mathbf{x}_0-\mathbf{p})\cdot\mathbf{n}|}{\|\mathbf{n}\|} \).

- The vector \( \mathbf{x}_0-\mathbf{p} \) points from the plane to the point.
- Only its component in the normal direction contributes to perpendicular distance from the plane.
- The signed scalar component along \( \mathbf{n} \) is

\[ \frac{(\mathbf{x}_0-\mathbf{p})\cdot\mathbf{n}}{\|\mathbf{n}\|}. \]

- Taking absolute value gives the nonnegative distance:

\[
\operatorname{dist}(\mathbf{x}_0,\Pi)
=
\frac{|(\mathbf{x}_0-\mathbf{p})\cdot\mathbf{n}|}{\|\mathbf{n}\|}.
\]

![The foot of the perpendicular from x0 to the plane: only the component of x0 - p along n contributes to the distance](img/distance_point_plane.svg)
~~~

!id: 5kCFngzGoO
!front: How do normal vectors determine the angle between two planes?
!back:
If two planes have nonzero normal vectors \( \mathbf{n}_1 \) and \( \mathbf{n}_2 \), then the angle between the planes is the acute angle between their normal directions:

\[
\cos\theta
=
\frac{|\mathbf{n}_1\cdot\mathbf{n}_2|}{\|\mathbf{n}_1\|\,\|\mathbf{n}_2\|},
\qquad
0\le\theta\le\frac{\pi}{2}.
\]

The absolute value is used because replacing a normal vector by its negative does not change the plane.
~~~

!id: MrcFfqs8E4
!front: How is the angle between a line and a plane related to the line's direction vector and the plane's normal vector?
!back:
Let \( \phi \) be the acute angle between the line and the plane, where the line has nonzero direction vector \( \mathbf{d} \) and the plane has nonzero normal vector \( \mathbf{n} \). Since \( \mathbf{n} \) is perpendicular to the plane, \( \phi \) is complementary to the acute angle between \( \mathbf{d} \) and \( \mathbf{n} \). Thus

\[
\sin\phi
=
\frac{|\mathbf{d}\cdot\mathbf{n}|}{\|\mathbf{d}\|\,\|\mathbf{n}\|},
\qquad
0\le\phi\le\frac{\pi}{2}.
\]

In particular, \( \phi=0 \) exactly when \( \mathbf{d}\cdot\mathbf{n}=0 \), so the line is parallel to the plane.
~~~
